From 9a63ca66d2656197121b5ed8889131a8e6125dab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 08:25:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] God rays and morning mist: light shafts through Elwynn's
 trees, mist in the hollows at dawn

The new look's full-screen pass (0053) gains two effects, both at the strengths Elwynn's grade
gives the hour (0051): the rays strongest at first light and golden hour and gone at night, the
mist thickest at dawn and thin at noon.

God rays are screen-space light shafts (GPU Gems 3, chapter 13): the sun (the visible one, the
direction the sun disc is drawn in) is projected to the screen, and each pixel near it marches 40
samples toward it over the depth buffer; a sample that is open sky (reverse-Z far, the depth the
sky dome never writes) lets the light through, weighted by a decay, so trees and roofs cut the
light into shafts. They are added in the sun's own colour and fade, never cut, with the sun low
or under the horizon, as it leaves the screen and as it goes behind the camera.

The mist is an exponential height fog: densest at its base, 2 yd over the player's feet, thinning
by e every 6 yd up, integrated along each view ray in closed form, so a hollow below fills white
while a hilltop stays clear. It takes the zone's fog colour and brightens toward the sun's side,
skips the sky (its dome has its own), and never covers more than 85% of a pixel. The pass reads
the camera's depth buffer, made sampleable while the new look is on, single-sampled or
multisampled (`gxMultisample`), each its own pipeline. The sun's projection and fades and the
mist's integral (against a numeric one) are tested on the CPU, and naga validates and translates
the shader in both variants.

`LookMistTune` holds the mist at a multiple of the hour's own, for filming a range from subtle to
the densest still playable (the console's `lookmist`).
